{
  "difficulty": "easy",
  "score": 3,
  "estimated_minutes": 20,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The reviewer needs only the straightforward sequence of an individual employment arbitration agreement, a disability-discrimination charge, a right-to-sue letter, and the employer's motion to compel arbitration."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The opinion itself explains both the individual-agreement versus collective-bargaining waiver distinction and the statutory effect of the Commission's right-to-sue letter."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The relevant language is concentrated in paragraph 1's arbitration mandate, covered-claims list, and two exclusions, without requiring reconstruction from other agreements or remote provisions."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "Sections III.A–C expressly resolve statutory-claim coverage, the Commission exclusion, and the allocation of legal and equitable relief between arbitration and court."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "d01 and d02 share the same statutory-coverage dispute and opinion passage, so the reviewer must distinguish the broad employment nexus from the discrimination list and state-statute catch-all supporting that coverage."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d04: The reviewer must distinguish excluding equitable relief from excluding the entire mixed-remedy action, and understand that liability and legal damages go to arbitration while equitable relief remains for later court consideration without relitigating the merits.",
  "reason": "This is suitable for a general law-trained reviewer with a short orientation to the procedural sequence. The court quotes the operative language and explains its constructions directly, so outside research into employment discrimination or arbitration law should not be necessary. The main care points are the overlapping but textually different coverage defects d01 and d02, and the remedy-specific split under d04. A focused reading of paragraph 1, the background, Sections III.A–C, and the no-relitigation footnote should produce confident recognition of the defects."
}
